"K. Bailey" wrote:
> 
>   The "oR is ... just too good a reward for the effort.

	I think it's too good... for experienced players.  For
inexperienced players, it doesn't make a noticable difference:  
they die anyway, usually long before Sokoban.  (You've seen the
"found a WoW ...  died two levels later" posts.)  It only makes a
big difference to players who are almost ready to ascend.  

	(I've got to admit that "ignore Sokoban" wasn't a challenge
that occurred to me until just now.)

	We've got an awful lot of experienced players who are
using all sorts of bizarre challenges to maintain their interest
in the game by cranking up the game's difficulty.  I think that 
the proper solution is to re-introduce what the Dev Team took out 
a while ago:  difficulty levels.

	Proposal for Expert difficulty level:

   o  Wands of Wishing have only one charge, and cannot be recharged.

   o  Sokoban's loot gets replaced by six or eight random items.

   o  Gehennom's random-maze levels are left out.  (Been there, done
      that.)

	I can't help but notice that most of the above can be done
by level designers.  (Replace the castle WoW by 2-3 blessed magic
lamps to fake the first one.)

> The bag of holding I don't mind since I hate inventory
> management.

	I find that digging up a bag can be a nice challenge in 
the mid-game.  If you think that killing the AoR is a good idea, 
don't feel mercy for the other AK item.  :-)

> So solutions might include:
> 
> 1. Make sokoban harder.

	This does not address giving away a guaranteed AK item.  
> 
> 2. Replace the amulet or reduce its probability
> by having more reward level possibilities. Magic
> marker? Wand of polymorph? Nice potions?

	If you're dragging away the AK item, don't replace it by
